const content="<article class=\"sn-page\" data-editorial-version=\"4\"><div class=\"sn-read\"><a href=\"/supporters/\">Featured sponsors</a><p class=\"sn-kicker\">Marine Diesel Engine \u00b7 Sponsor feature</p><img class=\"sn-logo\" src=\"/images/supporters/poclink/logo.png\" alt=\"Poclink logo\" width=\"344\" height=\"266\" loading=\"lazy\"><h1>Poclink radios for the marine workshop and the parts run</h1><p class=\"sn-intro\">A mechanic has the old component on the bench. The parts runner is at the marina entrance and the service coordinator has the delivery docket. Poclink handheld radios let a shore team speak over mobile networks at the press of a button. A single update can tell all three where the replacement needs to go.</p><img class=\"sn-hero\" src=\"/images/supporters/poclink/photo.webp\" alt=\"Push-to-talk radios from Poclink\" width=\"900\" height=\"900\" loading=\"lazy\"><section><h2>Give the job a channel</h2><p>Group calls suit messages like \u201cthe courier is at gate two\u201d. A private call suits a detail about one customer&#x27;s collection. That&#x27;s the distinction we like for a marine workshop: people can keep listening for the updates that affect their work without hearing every discussion about an invoice. A handset can also pass from the morning coordinator to the afternoon one without changing anybody&#x27;s contact details.</p></section><img class=\"sn-hero\" src=\"/images/supporters/poclink/detail.webp\" alt=\"Poclink POC-2 Titan radio\" width=\"1000\" height=\"428\" loading=\"lazy\"><section><h2>Check the difficult end of the yard</h2><p>The POC-1 Lite operates over 4G. The Pro can also use Wi-Fi. In a metal workshop with usable wireless coverage but patchy mobile reception, that second connection could make a difference. Try a pair with one person beside the parts counter and another at the farthest service berth. Give Poclink the Australian locations you cover and confirm the applicable cellular service terms.</p><p>We&#x27;d agree where the handsets live overnight and who takes the spare. Their own batteries let the team coordinate even if the boat is isolated for maintenance. Use the vessel&#x27;s appropriate maritime communications equipment when afloat; the job these handsets do especially well is keeping the people ashore in touch through a busy service day.</p></section><p><a rel=\"sponsored nofollow noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://poclink.com/\">Compare Poclink radios for your team \u2197</a></p></div></article>";
